== Case text (Ask-CK ck.db zephyr_cases, read-only)
Case:          AWPTCM-T18570  (Approved)
Title:         802.1Q Trunk a Static Aggreator
Objective:     To confirm that the static aggreagator will work as normally as a trunked port
Precondition:  change the configuration such that the trunked port
Step 1 text (verbatim):     The 802.1Q trunked port should work equally as well being a static Aggregator. Multiple source mac addresses should be sent from the Ixia
Step 1 test data:           (empty)
Step 1 expected (verbatim): There should be no 4 byte deficit per frame in traffic flow

== Method
No Ixia on tb470: tools/linerate.py (tcpreplay) on tb470's 1G NICs stands in, with 64 rotating source MACs per sender.
Topology: tb470 eth3 -> x230 port1.0.5 (access VLAN 106) -> x230 static aggregator sa2 = port1.0.2 + port1.0.3, an
802.1Q trunk carrying VLAN 106 tagged (native VLAN 1) -> stack static aggregator sa2 = port1.0.2 + port1.0.17 (+ the
existing non-present member port1.0.9), same trunk -> stack port3.0.13 (access VLAN 106) -> tb470 eth1.
"No 4 byte deficit per frame": frames of the maximum standard untagged size (1518 bytes on the wire) gain the 4-byte
802.1Q tag on the aggregated trunk (1522 bytes) and must leave the far access port at exactly the length sent; every
receive counter uses --rx-len (counts only frames of exactly the sent length). Line rate (top speed) both ways, plus
1500-byte frames, with the x230's per-member counters to show the aggregator spreads the source MACs over both links.
The destination MAC is never learned (02:00:00:00:99:99 / :98), so frames flood VLAN 106, which holds only the two access
ports and the aggregators; TTL 1, so tb470 cannot forward anything it receives.

